<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Trinidad and Tobago</h1><p class="st-meta">1962 CE – present · capital Port of Spain</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Twin-island Caribbean state, independent since 1962 and a republic since 1976, built on oil and gas and known as the home of Carnival, calypso and steelpan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-indies-federation">West Indies Federation</a> collapsed, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-trinidad-and-tobago">Colony of Trinidad and Tobago</a> became independent on 31 August 1962 under Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-williams">Eric Williams</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-movement">People's National Movement</a>, with the British monarch as head of state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-williams">Williams</a> governed until his death in 1981. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-power-revolution">Black Power</a> protests of 1970 and an army mutiny shook his government, but the oil boom of the 1970s brought wealth and nationalisation of the energy sector. A new constitution made the country a republic on 1 August 1976, with a president as head of state. Politics has tended to divide along African and Indian lines, and natural gas and petrochemicals became the backbone of the economy.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaat-al-muslimeen">Jamaat al Muslimeen</a> held the prime minister and parliament hostage in a failed coup attempt in 1990. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basdeo-panday">Basdeo Panday</a> became the first prime minister of Indian descent in 1995 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamla-persad-bissessar">Kamla Persad-Bissessar</a> the first woman in 2010. The country remains a parliamentary republic.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/trinidad-and-tobago.jpg" alt="Trinidad and Tobago" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: See File history, below, for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Trinidad_and_Tobago.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1962 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port of Spain</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eric Williams, A. N. R. Robinson, Basdeo Panday</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Hinduism and Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Trinidadian Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Trinidad and Tobago</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1994&amp;focus=trinidad-and-tobago" aria-label="Trinidad and Tobago on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2985 1780 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Trinidad and Tobago lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3305 2036L3305 2036"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3305 2036h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3305 2036h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3305 2036h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Port of Spain. <a href="/atlas?year=1994&amp;focus=trinidad-and-tobago">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
